    changes won't persist. Never a blank page.
  - [1mCorrupt JSON[0m: don't throw. Copy the unreadable payload to a backup key before starting empty — silently destroying
    someone's tasks is the worst failure available here.
  - [1m[38;5;153mschemaVersion[39m newer than we know[0m: refuse to write, warn, stay read-only.
  - [1mUnparseable [38;5;153mdueAt[0m: treat as [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  [1mTesting[0m

  Runner is node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m — zero dependencies, consistent with having no build step. Coverage targets the
  pure modules: boundary cases in [38;5;153mevaluate[39m ([38;5;153mdueAt[39m exactly equal to [38;5;153mnow[39m, both edges of the due-soon window, completed
  excluded, snoozed excluded, snooze expiry re-surfacing the task), transition detection (first evaluation fires
  nothing; one crossing fires exactly one toast), and [38;5;153mstore.js[39m against a fake localStorage for the corrupt-JSON and
  quota paths. The UI modules aren't unit tested — that's the trade for skipping e2e, and it means "does a toast
  visually appear" stays a manual check.

  [1mBuild order[0m

  [1mPhase 1[0m — store, tasks, list UI, add/complete/delete with a due date. A working task app on its own. [1mPhase 2[0m — clock,
  evaluator, tick loop, toasts, snooze.

  [1mExplicitly out of scope[0m

  Notification history and read-state (that's approach B), recurring tasks, cross-tab sync, multi-user, server push.
  Also the [1mOS-level Notification API[0m: it only works while the tab is open anyway, and it costs a permission prompt for
  marginal gain over an in-page toast. It's an easy add later if you want it.
